  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nBroad cyclonic synoptic flow in place across the Northeast US \ntoday helping to keep the active weather in place. A pre-frontal\ntrough is rotating through central NY/PA this morning and \ncombining with a modest amount of deep moisture (PWs around 1.5 \ninches) to produce mainly rain showers, but also an area of \nthunderstorms tracking east over the northern Finger Lakes. \nThere is a sufficient amount of instability (ML CAPE values \naround 500-1000 J/kg) for this line of convection to tap into, \nbut the limited forcing from the short wave and minimal amount \nof deep layer shear is keeping the convection on the weaker \nside. \n\nAs this line of convection tracks east and the morning \ncontinues to heat up, the boundary layer will destabilize \nfurther and allow for additional convective initiation and \ndeeper, potentially more organized storm structure. SPC has \nexpanded the Marginal Risk for severe storms farther north and \nalso place parts of Pike County PA and Sullivan County NY in the\nSlight Risk category. \n\nThe primary concern will be for heavy rain and training \nconvection that could potentially produce flash flooding, \nespecially in locations that have seen significant rain the past\nseveral days. Pockets of PWs above 1.5 inches, warm cloud depth\nabove 10kft, short MBE vectors and the potential for storms to \nmove parallel to boundary dragging across the region will all \ncontribute to the threat for isolated areas of heavy \nrain/training storms and flash flooding.\n\nThere is also a concern for damaging winds from downbursts in \nany of these storms that generate enough vertical depth, or \nbecome organized due to localized storm-induced shear profiles. \n\nMost of the convection will be focused along this pre-frontal \ntrough as the primary cold front hangs back well to the west and\ndoes not move through until later this evening/tonight. The \nbulk of the strong to severe storms will likely impact the \nregion before 5-7 PM this evening, with lingering rain showers \nand possible a few weak storms after 7 PM and nearly all \nprecipitation done after 1 AM.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWeak high pressure builds in from the west tonight, which will \nallow for some clearing to occur along with winds \ndiminishing...leading to enough raditional cooling to generate \npatch fog. After the fog mixes out on Sunday morning, the rest \nof the day should remain dry and quiet. \n\nThe early part of Monday should be mostly quiet as well, but \nthe next round of showers and storms looks to move in from the \nwest later in the day Monday. There is some indication that the \nremnants of a Midwest MCS could be tracking east through the \nsouthern Great Lakes Sunday night/Monday morning and eventually \ninto NY/PA by Monday afternoon. An embedded upper short wave \nalong with this convective system may help sustain the storms as\nthey track to the east. This will require close watching as \nthere could be remnants of an EML associated with this \nconvection as well. \n\nChances for showers and storms Tuesday and Wednesday appear to \nbe on the lower side, but given the warm temperatures and high \nsurface dew points, persistent cyclonic flow and sporadic weak \nshort waves within the flow, any one period cannot be ruled out \ncompletely for thunderstorms. \n\nA higher chance for showers and storms arrives Thursday with \nthe arrival of a deep upper trough and associated stronger \nforcing. The weekend may be cooler and drier if this trough can \nkick east in time.",
